        'en' => [
            'title'   => 'Laser engraving stone and slate: granite, marble and tiles',
            'slug'    => 'laser-engraving-stone-slate',
            'excerpt' => 'Laser engraving stone and slate: which stones give the best contrast, which xTool laser to choose for slate, granite and tiles, and what a laser cannot do.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Laser engraving stone does not carve deep letters – it lightens and frosts the surface. Dark, fine-grained stone such as slate and polished black granite gives the best contrast, while on light marble the mark is barely visible. A diode laser is enough for dark stone, a CO₂ laser marks almost any stone and large slabs, and a UV laser suits ceramics and fine detail.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Below we explain what happens to stone under the beam, when to choose the xTool P3, S1, F2 or F2 Ultra UV, how to engrave uneven pebbles and heavy slabs, how to deal with dust and which stone products you can sell. We are also clear about what a desktop laser cannot do with stone.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'What does a laser do to stone?' ],
                [ 'p', 'The beam heats a thin surface layer very quickly. At that spot the stone breaks up into microscopic fragments and some of the material vaporises. What remains is a rough, matte surface that scatters light and therefore looks lighter than the rest of the stone – much like frosted glass. The laser adds no colour: you get a light mark, and how readable it is depends on how strongly it stands out from the background.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Four properties of the stone decide the contrast:' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Colour. The darker the background, the clearer the light mark. Slate, black granite and dark pebbles give the best result, white marble the weakest.',
                    'Grain. Fine-grained, uniform stone such as slate holds small lettering and thin lines well. In coarse-grained granite, large crystals break detail up into speckles.',
                    'Finish. A polished surface looks darker than a matte one, so the mark stands out more. On raw, rough stone the difference is smaller.',
                    'Uniformity. Veins, inclusions and layers react to the beam differently from the rest of the stone, so two pieces from the same box can give different results.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'Always run a test on an offcut or on the underside of the item before a batch. Stone is a natural material and every piece is slightly different.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'What can’t a desktop laser do with stone?' ],
                [ 'p', 'A laser engraves stone shallowly: more passes deepen the mark but add time, and the result still never looks like a chiselled letter. Hence a few limits:' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'It will not carve headstone lettering. Deep, tactile inscriptions on memorials are made by sandblasting through a stencil or by CNC milling; a desktop laser leaves a shallow, light mark.',
                    'It will not cut stone. Slabs, slate and tiles are cut with a diamond saw or a waterjet; the laser only engraves their surface.',
                    'It will not add colour. The mark is always light; for colour, fill the engraving with paint.',
                    'It will not fit every slab. The xTool P3 bed is 914 × 457 mm and the S1 bed 608 × 385 mm. Window sills, worktops and gravestones are a job for a stonemason.',
                ] ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which laser for engraving stone: CO₂, diode or UV?' ],
                [ 'h3', 'CO₂ laser: versatile and suited to large formats (xTool P3)' ],
                [ 'p', 'A CO₂ laser works at a wavelength of 10.6 µm. Stone, glass and ceramics absorb it well, and the colour of the surface matters little. That is why CO₂ also marks light marble and white glaze, although a light mark on a light background will still be hard to see.' ],
                [ 'p', 'The xTool P3 has an 80 W CO₂ laser, a 914 × 457 mm bed and speeds of up to 1,200 mm/s, so it takes a large slate sign or a whole batch of coasters. Four cameras help you place the design on the material, and the ACS™ smart workflow covers auto-positioning, autofocus, LiDAR distance measurement and an auto-lifting base.' ],
                [ 'products', '9089720189141' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Diode laser: slate and dark granite (xTool S1 and F2)' ],
                [ 'p', 'A diode laser emits blue light at around 455 nm. Dark stone absorbs it well, so a diode leaves a clear mark on slate, black granite and dark pebbles. Light marble and white glaze reflect most of that light and react poorly – for those, reach for CO₂, and for ceramics, UV.' ],
                [ 'p', 'The xTool S1 is an enclosed Class 1 diode laser with a 20 W or 40 W module and a 608 × 385 mm bed. It offers Pin-point™ positioning, one-click autofocus and 3D Curve™ engraving on curved surfaces, and the enclosure keeps dust inside, where it is easy to extract. The portable xTool F2 pairs a 15 W diode laser with a 5 W IR laser for metals and plastics, has a 50 MP camera with manually refreshed preview and engraves at up to 6,000 mm/s. The F2 is a Class 4 professional laser: xTool requires training and laser safety officer oversight and prohibits use in public or around untrained people or children.' ],
                [ 'products', '8169201631445,9163846877397' ],
                [ 'h3', 'UV laser: ceramics and fine detail (xTool F2 Ultra UV)' ],
                [ 'p', 'A UV laser has the shortest wavelength, typically 355 nm, and works by cold processing: it removes material without scorching around the mark. The xTool F2 Ultra UV with its 5 W UV laser lists ceramics among its supported materials and has two 48 MP cameras and speeds of up to 15,000 mm/s. Choose it for glazed tiles, fine lettering on small stones and ceramic gifts. At 5 W, though, it is no substitute for a CO₂ laser on large, densely filled designs on slate.' ],
                [ 'products', '9187692970197' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Comparison: which stone with which laser' ],
                [ 'p', 'Treat the table as a starting point for a test, not a guaranteed result – stone from a different quarry can react differently.' ],
                [ 'table', [ 'Material', 'Contrast', 'Diode (S1, F2)', 'CO₂ (P3)', 'UV (F2 Ultra UV)' ], [
